  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Friday/...\nRain chances will continue across the eastern half of Arizona during \nthe middle of the week as Pacific troughing lingers just to our \nnorth and northwest, and ridging stays centered over the southeast \nConus. This will continue place Arizona in southwest flow providing \nperiods of moisture advection out of northern Mexico. The biggest \nquestion that remains is what day, or days, will represent the best \nchances for more than just scattered showers and isolated storms. \nModels agree plentiful moisture will remain over eastern and central \nArizona through at least the middle of next week with PWATs running \n150-180% of normal and low level mixing ratios anywhere from 11-14 \ng/kg. However, there are uncertainties with the amount of \ninstability due to forecast cloud cover and lack of any obvious \ntrigger mechanisms. Last night's model runs were more optimistic \nwith upper level support, and this will likely be a piece of the \npuzzle needed for an active weather day given the questionable \ninstability and inhibition. \n\nThe NBM is still honing in on early Tuesday as the best potential \nfor showers and thunderstorms across eastern and central Arizona, \nbut confidence in this is still fairly low. It would not be \nsurprising to have any day Tuesday through Thursday to be a good \nstorm day, but it is still too early to pinpoint which day or days. \nWith the upper level support leaning toward being less of an \ninfluence, we may just have to rely on differential heating and/or \nwherever the western edge of the moisture axis sets up. Forecast \ninstability does look to be enough to support some strong storms and \nwith the moisture levels expected, heavy rainfall leading to \nlocalized flooding could also become an issue. Overall, rain chances \nfor next week look quite conditional and PoPs are likely to change. \nForecast temperatures for next week have also been trending upward \nover the past few days with decreasing chances of having lower \ndesert highs dipping below 100 degrees on any day. The latest NBM \nshows highs hovering right around normal to a few degrees above.",
